  "body": {
    "id": "prop:ceiling-sensitivity-monotonicity",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For every \\(\\bar L\\ge L\\) and every \\(\\mathbf z\\) compatible under ceiling \\(L\\), \\(\\mathcal I_L(\\mathbf z)\\subseteq\\mathcal I_{\\bar L}(\\mathbf z)\\) and \\[\\inf\\mathcal I_{\\bar L}(\\mathbf z)\\le D_1=\\inf\\mathcal I_L(\\mathbf z)\\le U_1=\\sup\\mathcal I_L(\\mathbf z)\\le\\sup\\mathcal I_{\\bar L}(\\mathbf z).\\] Therefore \\(U_1-D_1\\le\\sup\\mathcal I_{\\bar L}(\\mathbf z)-\\inf\\mathcal I_{\\bar L}(\\mathbf z)\\). The same nesting holds for the separable-direct-effect sets after subtracting the factual quantity \\(F_1^0(T)\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(L\\)",
      "\\(\\bar L\\)",
      "\\(\\mathbf z\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal I_c(\\mathbf z)\\)",
      "\\(D_k\\)",
      "\\(U_k\\)",
      "\\(F_1^0(T)\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:measurable-hazards",
      "ass:bounded-hazards",
      "ass:cause-one-splicing",
      "ass:cause-two-splicing",
      "ass:factual-law-reproduction",
      "def:ceiling-indexed-identified-set",
      "def:global-endpoints",
      "thm:global-sharp-recursion"
    ],
    "justification": "The ceiling determines the feasible hazard class, so nesting under a conservative larger ceiling is the minimum transparent sensitivity guarantee.",
    "gap": "MartinussenStensrud2023 uses resolved hazards to identify the cross-arm integral, but it does not compare sharp grouped-data endpoints across alternative numerical intensity ceilings.",
    "consumer": "Analysts can report the primary interval at an externally calibrated ceiling and a conservative interval at \\(\\bar L\\), with guaranteed nesting rather than an informal robustness comparison.",
    "proof_tex": "Let \\(\\bar L\\ge L\\), and fix a law \\(\\mathbf z\\) compatible under ceiling \\(L\\).  Every measurable hazard quadruple taking values in \\([0,L]^4\\) also takes values in \\([0,\\bar L]^4\\).  The splicing equations and factual law are unchanged when the admissible ceiling is enlarged.  The definition \\(\\mathrm{def{:}ceiling\\text{-}indexed\\text{-}identified\\text{-}set}\\) therefore gives the set inclusion\n\\[\n \\mathcal I_L(\\mathbf z)\\subseteq\\mathcal I_{\\bar L}(\\mathbf z).  \\tag{21}\n\\]\nBoth sets are nonempty because the smaller one is nonempty.  Taking infima and suprema in (21), and using \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}global\\text{-}sharp\\text{-}recursion}\\) at ceiling \\(L\\), yields\n\\[\n \\inf\\mathcal I_{\\bar L}(\\mathbf z)\n \\le \\inf\\mathcal I_L(\\mathbf z)=D_1\n \\le U_1=\\sup\\mathcal I_L(\\mathbf z)\n \\le \\sup\\mathcal I_{\\bar L}(\\mathbf z).                  \\tag{22}\n\\]\nSubtracting the first term in (22) from the last and comparing the middle difference gives the claimed width inequality.  By (20) in \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}global\\text{-}sharp\\text{-}recursion}\\), \\(F_1^0(T)\\) is a fixed observable functional of \\(\\mathbf z\\).  Translation of both sets in (21) by this same number preserves inclusion and widths, proving the separable-direct-effect assertion.",
